The private keys of the test certificates of surf-tls-test-certs: PKCS#8, DER. Made once on
2026-11-22 with OpenSSL 3.6.5 (29 Sep 2026), never regenerated: a certificate made from them is
reproducible only while they stay. They are test keys; nothing trusts them.

Each file was written by one of these commands, run in this directory:

  openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:4096 -outform DER -out rsa4096.pk8
  openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -outform DER -out rsa2048-<i>.pk8   (i = 0, 1)
  openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:1024 -outform DER -out rsa1024.pk8
  openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-384 -outform DER -out p384-<i>.pk8   (i = 0, 1, 2)
  openssl genpkey -algorithm ed25519 -outform DER -out ed25519-<i>.pk8   (i = 0, 1, 2)
  openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -outform DER -out p256-<nn>.pk8   (nn = 00 to 59)

p256-60.pk8 was made later, on 2026-11-22 with the same OpenSSL, by the same P-256 command
(nn = 60) and the same conversion below: the leaf key of ok-wildcard, the one P-256 leaf whose key
no other case shares, so that a test can bind CertificateVerify to the leaf's key.

genpkey -outform DER wrote the RSA keys as PKCS#1 and the EC keys as SEC1 (measured with asn1parse),
so each rsa*, p256-* and p384-* file was then turned into PKCS#8 in place, the key unchanged:

  openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt -inform DER -in <file> -outform DER -out <file>.new && mv <file>.new <file>

openssl writes Ed25519 as PKCS#8 v1, which the seam reads with
Ed25519KeyPair::from_pkcs8_maybe_unchecked (docs/design/tls.md, item 6). ring signs with none of
rsa1024.pk8 (a modulus under 2048 bits): only openssl/make.sh reads it.

